WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.025
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:08.677
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.977 --> 00:00:16.436
Unit seventeen: Climate Science Policy. Today you will distinguish concepts using differs from / distinguishing between.

4
00:00:16.736 --> 00:00:19.318
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.511
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.350 --> 00:00:25.792
Climate policy.

7
00:00:26.783 --> 00:00:31.395
Climate policy means government action to limit and respond to climate change.

8
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:35.759
Climate policy now shapes energy and transport decisions.

9
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.792
Your turn. Say: Climate policy.

10
00:00:42.633 --> 00:00:43.901
Climate policy.

11
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:45.686
Mitigation.

12
00:00:46.683 --> 00:00:50.517
Mitigation means actions that reduce the causes of climate change.

13
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:55.102
Mitigation includes cutting emissions and protecting forests.

14
00:00:55.717 --> 00:00:58.183
Your turn. Say: Mitigation.

15
00:01:01.033 --> 00:01:02.290
Mitigation.

16
00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:04.237
Adaptation.

17
00:01:05.250 --> 00:01:08.817
Adaptation means adjusting to the effects of climate change.

18
00:01:09.417 --> 00:01:12.891
Adaptation includes sea walls and drought-resistant crops.

19
00:01:13.483 --> 00:01:16.031
Your turn. Say: Adaptation.

20
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.100
Adaptation.

21
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:22.339
Emissions target.

22
00:01:23.350 --> 00:01:28.055
Emissions target means a goal for reducing greenhouse gas output by a set date.

23
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.787
The country set an emissions target for 2040.

24
00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:35.256
Your turn. Say: Emissions target.

25
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:39.461
Emissions target.

26
00:01:40.217 --> 00:01:41.241
Climate model.

27
00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.596
Climate model means a computer simulation of the climate system.

28
00:01:46.183 --> 00:01:48.868
A climate model projected the rise in sea level.

29
00:01:49.483 --> 00:01:52.832
Your turn. Say: Climate model.

30
00:01:55.667 --> 00:01:56.517
Climate model.

31
00:01:57.283 --> 00:01:58.319
Tipping point.

32
00:01:59.317 --> 00:02:03.510
Tipping point means a threshold beyond which change becomes self-reinforcing.

33
00:02:04.117 --> 00:02:06.697
Melting ice sheets may pass a tipping point.

34
00:02:07.283 --> 00:02:09.669
Your turn. Say: Tipping point.

35
00:02:12.533 --> 00:02:13.651
Tipping point.

36
00:02:14.417 --> 00:02:16.021
International agreement.

37
00:02:17.017 --> 00:02:20.711
International agreement means a formal accord between countries.

38
00:02:21.317 --> 00:02:24.013
The international agreement set shared goals.

39
00:02:24.617 --> 00:02:27.803
Your turn. Say: International agreement.

40
00:02:30.667 --> 00:02:32.376
International agreement.

41
00:02:33.117 --> 00:02:34.420
Resilience.

42
00:02:35.417 --> 00:02:38.891
Resilience means the ability to absorb shocks and recover.

43
00:02:39.483 --> 00:02:42.493
Resilience protects communities after floods.

44
00:02:43.083 --> 00:02:45.829
Your turn. Say: Resilience.

45
00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:49.854
Resilience.

46
00:02:50.667 --> 00:02:54.387
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:55.250 --> 00:03:00.317
Visal, my town is building flood walls. Isn't the real goal to stop climate change?

48
00:03:01.417 --> 00:03:10.187
That's the distinction between mitigation and adaptation. Mitigation differs from adaptation in its core goal.

49
00:03:11.283 --> 00:03:12.644
How exactly?

50
00:03:13.750 --> 00:03:22.611
Mitigation reduces the causes by cutting emissions, whereas adaptation reduces the harm by adjusting to effects already underway.

51
00:03:23.717 --> 00:03:25.020
So we need both?

52
00:03:26.117 --> 00:03:35.236
Yes. Distinguishing between the two helps allocate funds, and a climate model can show how much of each a region needs.

53
00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.249
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.083 --> 00:03:42.906
Grammar focus: Distinguishing between X and Y.

55
00:03:43.206 --> 00:03:44.346
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.050 --> 00:03:48.292
Mitigation differs from adaptation in its core goal.

57
00:03:49.183 --> 00:03:52.147
Analysts distinguish between causes and effects.

58
00:03:53.050 --> 00:03:56.466
Distinguishing between the two helps allocate funds wisely.

59
00:03:57.400 --> 00:04:05.648
distinguish between X and Y (not 'distinguish X with Y'); X differs from Y in + respect. Gerund subject: Distinguishing between them helps…

60
00:04:06.383 --> 00:04:10.812
Be careful! Don't say: Mitigation differs adaptation in goal.

61
00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:16.589
Say: Mitigation differs from adaptation in goal.

62
00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:17.751
differ from.

63
00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:21.277
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:22.067 --> 00:04:23.126
Cut or Cope?.

65
00:04:23.426 --> 00:04:26.900
Climate policy rests on two complementary strategies.

66
00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:37.040
Mitigation reduces the causes of warming by cutting emissions and protecting carbon sinks, whereas adaptation prepares societies for effects that can no longer be avoided.

67
00:04:38.033 --> 00:04:46.012
Mitigation differs from adaptation in its core goal, and in who benefits: cuts help everyone, but sea walls protect only one coast.

68
00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:51.763
Climate models suggest that crossing a tipping point could make some changes irreversible.

69
00:04:52.767 --> 00:04:57.727
Distinguishing between the two strategies, and funding both, is the foundation of resilience.

70
00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:02.580
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.417 --> 00:05:06.461
Mitigation differs blank adaptation in its core goal.

72
00:05:06.761 --> 00:05:09.451
A, from. B, of. C, with.

73
00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:14.489
The answer is A: from.

74
00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.845
Actions that reduce the causes of climate change are blank.

75
00:05:19.145 --> 00:05:23.437
A, mitigation. B, adaptation. C, resilience.

76
00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:28.791
The answer is A: mitigation.

77
00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:33.482
A threshold beyond which change reinforces itself is a blank.

78
00:05:33.782 --> 00:05:37.842
A, climate model. B, tipping point. C, target.

79
00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:43.922
The answer is B: tipping point.

80
00:05:45.083 --> 00:05:46.804
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:47.104 --> 00:05:54.310
A, Distinguish between cause and effect.. B, Distinguish cause with effect.. C, Distinguish between cause with effect..

82
00:05:57.867 --> 00:06:01.329
The answer is A: Distinguish between cause and effect..

83
00:06:02.483 --> 00:06:05.156
According to the reading, sea walls protect…

84
00:06:05.456 --> 00:06:09.342
A, everyone. B, only one coast. C, no one.

85
00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.271
The answer is B: only one coast.

86
00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:20.001
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:24.846
What is the difference between climate mitigation and climate adaptation?

88
00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.243
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.543 --> 00:07:08.585
Mitigation means reducing the causes of climate change, mainly by cutting greenhouse gas emissions. Adaptation means adjusting to effects that are already happening or unavoidable, such as building flood defences or changing crops. Mitigation protects the whole planet over the long term, while adaptation protects particular places now. We need both.

90
00:07:08.885 --> 00:07:10.629
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:11.517 --> 00:07:16.340
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and distinguishing between x and y.

92
00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:22.903
Today's value is environmental stewardship. Care for nature as a trust for future generations.

93
00:07:23.203 --> 00:07:26.355
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:26.655 --> 00:07:30.108
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

